,"cited":"II Thess. 2,11-12; Ps. 37,6; Luc. 16, 20-22; Phil. 2, 41; Luc. 16,22","note":"Stellenangabe im Apparat der Ausgabe (Seite; Absatz ungefähr)"},{"id":"7153c911e8c4","v":"csel.ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","w":"ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":23,"p":[6,6],"r":[2,4],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"I Cor. 11, 3; Matth. 27, 6; Gen. 49,16; Gen. 49,17; II Thess. 2, 4; Ioh. 3,18; Luc. 14, 23","note":"Stellenangabe im Apparat der Ausgabe (Seite; Absatz ungefähr)"},{"id":"cea6126ac4f1","v":"csel.ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","w":"ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":23,"p":[11,11],"r":[1,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"Gen. 49,17-18; Ioh. 18, 6; Col. 3, 2; II Thess. 1, 11; Luc. 23, 50; Ps. 54,16; Matth. 26, 58; Matth. 16,18; Matth. 16, 18-19; Luc. 10, 33-34","note":"Stellenangabe im Apparat der Ausgabe (Seite; Absatz ungefähr)"},{"id":"e507efbab188","v":"csel.ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","w":"ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":28,"p":[6,6],"r":[3,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"I Cor. 2, 6; Hebr. 5,14; Hebr. 5, 12; II Thess. 3,1; Ps. 146,1; Esai. 9,1; Matth. 15, 22","note":"Stellenangabe im Apparat der Ausgabe (Seite; Absatz ungefähr)"},{"id":"4d7dc84cf8a3","v":"npnf.augustinus.confessiones","w":"augustinus.confessiones","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":11,"p":[5,5],"r":[2,1,2,7],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Augustin is always careful to distinguish between the certain truths of faith and doctrine which all may know, and the mysteries of Scripture which all have not the ability equally to apprehend. \"Among the things,\" he says (De Doctr. Christ. ii. 14), \"that are plainly laid down in Scripture, are to be found all matters that concern faith, and the manner of life.\" As to the Scriptures that are obscure, he is slow to come to conclusions, lest he should \"be deceived in them or deceive out of them.\" In his De Gen. ad Lit. i. 37, he gives a useful warning against forcing our own meaning on Scripture in doubtful questions, and, ibid. viii. 5, we have the memorable words: \"Melius est dubitare de rebus occultis, quam litigare de incertis.\" For examples of how careful he is in such matters not to go beyond what is written, see his answer to the question raised by Evodius,-a question which reminds us of certain modern speculations (see The Unseen Universe , arts. 61, 201, etc.),-whether the soul on departing from the body has not still a body of some kind, and at least some of the senses proper to a body; and also (Ep. clxiv.) his endeavours to unravel Evodius' difficulties as to Christ's preaching to the spirits in prison (1 Pet. iii. 18-21). Similarly, he says, as to the Antichrist of 2 Thess. ii. 1-7 (De Civ. Dei, xx. 19): \"I frankly confess I know not what he means. I will, nevertheless, mention such conjectures as I have heard or read.\" See notes, pp. 64 and 92, above."},{"id":"f7f5321dbfa7","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_civitate_dei","w":"augustinus.de_civitate_dei","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":517,"p":[0,0],"r":[1,9],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Isa. xi. 4; 2 Thess. i. 9."},{"id":"931ec6436a3e","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_civitate_dei","w":"augustinus.de_civitate_dei","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":560,"p":[0,0],"r":[2,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Thess. ii. 8."},{"id":"cd9122fae260","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_civitate_dei","w":"augustinus.de_civitate_dei","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":567,"p":[0,0],"r":[2,1,2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Thess. ii. 1-11. Whole passage given in the Latin. In ver. 3 refuga is used instead of the Vulgate's discessio."},{"id":"4674c71c4f71","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_peccatorum_meritis","w":"augustinus.de_peccatorum_meritis","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":56,"p":[0,0],"r":[3,2],"k":"quotation",